      "text": "We are given that $\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v} + \\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{w}$ and $\\mathbf{w} \\times \\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{v}.$ To find $\\mathbf{u} \\cdot (\\mathbf{v} \\times \\mathbf{w}),$ we can manipulate these given equations:\n\\begin{align*}\n\\mathbf{w} &= \\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v} + \\mathbf{u} && \\\\ \\\\\n\\mathbf{w} \\times \\mathbf{u} &= (\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v} + \\mathbf{u}) \\times \\mathbf{u} &= \\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v} \\times \\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{u} && \\\\\n\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{u} &= \\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{0} && \\\\\n\\mathbf{w} \\times \\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{v} && \\\\\n\\end{align*} We are now ready to compute $\\mathbf{u} \\cdot (\\mathbf{v} \\times \\mathbf{w}).$ Using the scalar triple product definition of the dot product (see boz3r1.frm for more information), \\begin{align*}\n\\mathbf{u} \\cdot (\\mathbf{v} \\times \\mathbf{w}) &= \\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v} \\times (\\mathbf{v} \\times \\mathbf{w}) &\\\\ \n&= (\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v}) \\cdot \\mathbf{w} \\times \\mathbf{v} && \\\\\n&= (\\mathbf{u} \\cdot (\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v})) \\times (\\mathbf{v} \\times \\mathbf{v}) && \\\\\n&= (\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{0}) \\times (\\mathbf{v} \\times \\mathbf{0}) && \\\\\n&= 0 \\times 0 = \\boxed{0}\n\\end{align*} The final answer is: 0",
