      "text": "Given the problem, we know that $\\mathbf{w} = \\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v} + \\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{w} \\times \\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{v}.$ Substituting $\\mathbf{w}$ from the first equation into the second, we get $(\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v} + \\mathbf{u}) \\times \\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{v}.$  Expanding the left side using the distributive property of the cross product, we get $\\mathbf{u} \\times (\\mathbf{v} \\times \\mathbf{u}) + \\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{v}.$  Using the vector triple product identity $\\mathbf{u} \\times (\\mathbf{v} \\times \\mathbf{u}) = (\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{u})\\mathbf{v} - (\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v})\\mathbf{u},$ we find that the equation becomes $(\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{u})\\mathbf{v} - (\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v})\\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{0} = \\mathbf{v}.$  Since $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are unit vectors, $\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{u} = 1.$  So we can simplify the equation to $\\mathbf{v} - (\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v})\\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{0} = \\mathbf{v}.$  With a nice cancel-out, we get $\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v} = 1.$  Using this value in the equation we came up with (which was just the original second equation), we get $(\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v})\\mathbf{u} - (\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v})\\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{0} = \\mathbf{v}.$  Because $(\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v})\\mathbf{u} - (\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v})\\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{0},$ we conclude that $\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{v},$ so the vector $\\mathbf{v}$ satisfies the original equation.  This means that we are done.  Thus, $\\boxed{1}.$ The answer is 1",
