      "text": "We can first square both sides of the equation $\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v} + \\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{w}$ in order to obtain $\\mathbf{u} \\cdot (\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v}) + \\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{w} \\cdot \\mathbf{w},$ so\n\\[\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{u} \\mathbf{v} + 1 = \\mathbf{w} \\cdot \\mathbf{w}.\\]Then $\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{u}$ is $\\cos^2 \\theta,$ where $\\theta$ is the angle between $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}.$  Also, $\\mathbf{w} \\cdot \\mathbf{w} = \\|\\mathbf{w}\\|^2 = \\|\\mathbf{u}\\|^2 + \\|\\mathbf{v}\\|^2 + 2 \\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v} = 2 + 2 \\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v},$ so \\[\\cos^2 \\theta + 1 = 2 + 2 \\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v},\\]so \\[\\cos^2 \\theta = \\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v}.\\]Hence, $\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v} = \\cos^2 \\theta.$  A similar calculation verifies that $\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{w} = \\cos^2 \\theta.$  Finally, $\\cos^2 \\theta = \\| \\mathbf{u} \\| \\cos \\alpha,$ where $\\alpha$ is the angle between $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{w},$ which means that $\\mathbf{u} \\cdot (\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{w}) = \\|\\mathbf{u}\\| \\|\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{w}\\| \\cos \\alpha = \\|\\mathbf{u}\\| \\|\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{w}\\|.$  We can calculate this last quantity as follows:  \\begin{align*}\n\\|\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{w}\\| &= \\|\\mathbf{u} \\| \\|\\mathbf{w}\\| \\sin \\alpha \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2} \\sqrt{\\cos^2 \\theta + 1} \\sin \\alpha \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1 + 3 \\big( \\sin^2 \\alpha \\big)}.\n\\end{align*}The sine rule in triangle $\\mathbf{w} \\mathbf{v} \\mathbf{u}$ yields $\\sin \\alpha = \\frac{\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v}}{\\|\\mathbf{u}\\| \\|\\mathbf{v}\\|} = \\frac{\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v}}{\\sqrt{2}}.$  Therefore, $\\|\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{w}\\| = \\sqrt{2 + 3 \\big( \\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v} \\big)} = \\sqrt{2 + 3 \\cos^2 \\alpha},$ whence $\\mathbf{u} \\cdot (\\mathbf{v} \\times \\mathbf{w}) = \\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\big( \\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{w} \\big) = \\mathbf{u} \\cdot (\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{u}) - \\mathbf{u} \\cdot (\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v}) - \\mathbf{u} \\cdot (\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{w}).$  Compute each of these dot products separately:  \\begin{align*}\n\\mathbf{u} \\cdot (\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{u}) &= 0,\n\\end{align*}so we need only calculate $\\mathbf{u} \\cdot (\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v}) = \\textstyle \\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\left( \\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{v} \\right) \\times \\left( \\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{v} \\right) \\right) = \\frac{1}{2},$ since $\\frac{1}{2} \\mathbf{u} + \\frac{1}{2} \\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{u},$ and $u \\times u = \\mathbf{0}.$  Thus, $\\mathbf{u} \\cdot (\\mathbf{v} \\times \\mathbf{w}) = \\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}}.$ The answer is \\frac{1}{2}",
